    content: `The British journal *Philosophy* is completing its hundredth year in print. It marks the anniversary with a special issue devoted to an article published in its own pages a quarter of a century ago: Bernard Williams's **"Philosophy as a Humanistic Discipline"**.

## Williams's question

Williams's essay is short but bold. What it asks is this: is philosophy a science?

For much of the twentieth century, the implicit model of analytic philosophy was science. Philosophy was imagined as a discipline that progresses like physics, solves its problems and accumulates results.

Williams objects to that model. Philosophy, on his account, belongs to the same family as **history** and the **humanities** rather than to science. The reason is that our philosophical concepts — freedom, responsibility, justice, the self — are historically formed. To understand them is to understand how they came to be.

In science the past is usually a stage that has been superseded; nobody has to read Aristotle to understand present-day physics. In philosophy, Plato is still an interlocutor.

## The charge of scientism

Williams's position is a warning against the call for philosophy to model itself on science. When philosophy imitates science, he argues, it loses twice over: it cannot be as precise as science, and it abandons its own proper work — contributing to the human effort at self-understanding.

The argument is still live. On one side: experimental philosophy, naturalised epistemology and a philosophy of mind working closely with cognitive science. On the other: approaches that lean on conceptual history, literature and phenomenology.

## A hundred-year-old journal

*Philosophy* has been published since 1925 and is among the oldest philosophy journals in English. In an era of proliferating specialist titles, it is one of the few that still addresses a general philosophical readership.

Marking its centenary by returning to a text from its own past fits that line. As Williams said, in philosophy the past is not superseded; it is read again.`,
