    sources: [
      { title: "Prof. Catherine Malabou — On the Plasticity of the Unconditional", publisher: "Leiden University Institute for Philosophy", date: "9-10 February 2026", url: "https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/events/2026/02/institute-for-philosophy-prof.-malabou-on-the-plasticity-of-the-unconditional", primary: true },
    ],
    content: `The French philosopher Catherine Malabou received the title of *doctor honoris causa* from Leiden University on 9 February 2026. The next day she gave a public lecture at the university's Institute for Philosophy: *"On the Plasticity of the Unconditional"*.

## Where the lecture starts

Malabou began from Jacques Derrida's text *The University Without Condition* and then worked against it. Derrida had defined the university as an institution holding the right to question everything without submitting to any external condition: the university was the site of "unconditional resistance".

Malabou's question is this: can the university of today — the university under performance metrics, project funding, rankings and market pressure — still carry such unconditionality? Or must unconditionality itself change shape?

## Plasticity

The concept Malabou has worked on for thirty years is **plasticity**. The word cuts both ways: what is plastic both receives form and gives it — and, as in plastic explosive, can destroy form as well.

She drew the concept first out of a reading of Hegel (*The Future of Hegel*, 1996), then brought it together with neuroscience (*What Should We Do with Our Brain?*, 2004). That the brain is plastic — that it is reshaped by experience — is for her not merely a biological fact but a political question. Flexibility and plasticity are not the same thing: flexibility merely adapts to a form imposed from outside, while plasticity includes the power to give form.

What neoliberal work culture asks of people is flexibility; what Malabou defends is plasticity.

## Destructive plasticity

Her third layer is *destructive plasticity*: a new identity that emerges after an accident, a trauma or an illness and has no continuity with the old one. *Ontology of the Accident* (2009) works through that picture — against the psychoanalytic schema in which the repressed returns, an idea of transformation that does not return but breaks off completely.`,
