    title: "Burnt Herculaneum papyri read with AI: lost texts of Philodemus come to light",
    slug: "herculaneum-papyri-read-with-ai",
    summary:
      "The scrolls carbonised by the eruption of Vesuvius in AD 79 have been read without being opened, using X-ray imaging and artificial intelligence. Some 70 columns of 'On Vices' by the Epicurean philosopher Philodemus have been deciphered.",
    coverImage: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1461360370896-922624d12aa1?w=1600&q=80",
    imageCredit: "Illustrative image: Unsplash",
    featured: true,
    sourceName: "Philosophy Now, Issue 175",
    sourceUrl: "https://philosophynow.org/issues/175/News_August_September_2026",
    publishedAt: "2026-08-13T10:00:00.000Z",
    authorSlug: "world-desk",
    categorySlug: "world",
    tagSlugs: ["archaeology", "ancient-philosophy", "artificial-intelligence"],
    philosopherSlugs: [],
    content: `Scrolls that had been unreadable for close to two thousand years have been opened — without being opened at all. Advanced X-ray imaging and artificial intelligence have made it possible to "unwrap" digitally the papyri carbonised by the eruption of Vesuvius in AD 79.

## The Vesuvius Challenge

Launched in 2023, the international project set out to develop AI tools capable of reading the hundreds of carbonised scrolls found in the *Villa of the Papyri* at Herculaneum. So fragile are the scrolls that attempts to open them physically usually destroyed them beyond repair.

## What was found?

Among the deciphered texts are around 70 columns from Book 1 of *On Vices* by the Epicurean philosopher **Philodemus**, and passages from Book 8 of *On Gods*. Some 1.5 metres of text was also recovered from another scroll dated to between 300 and 200 BC — the oldest Herculaneum manuscript read to date, containing reflections on ethics, art and human nature.

## "A turning point"

Brent Seales, professor of computer science at the University of Kentucky and one of the project's founders, describes the development as a turning point and says the team believes every scroll in the collection can be read. Federica Nicolardi, the lead papyrologist, says virtual unwrapping has transformed the field: manuscripts can now be preserved and studied at the same time.

The project shares its imaging data, software and models as open source; a prize of one million dollars was offered to the first team to read a complete scroll. Hundreds of unopened scrolls are still waiting their turn.`,
