    content: `*Tempting Fate: A Philosophical Guide to Risk, Luck, and a Meaningful Life*, the new book by the epistemologist **Duncan Pritchard**, has been published by Princeton University Press. The 216-page book appeared in the United States on 25 August 2026 and reaches the United Kingdom on 20 October 2026.

## The thesis: risk is not a matter of probability

The theory Pritchard proposes explains risk **through fragility rather than probability**. What makes an action risky is not how likely the bad outcome is but how easily the good outcome could be undone.

The distinction makes a practical difference: the fragility framework can explain why skill and preparation are decisive even in an activity that is unavoidably high-risk.

## The example: El Capitan

The book's central example is the climber Alex Honnold's free solo ascent of El Capitan. Pritchard treats it as the exemplary case of "admirable risk-taking": years of preparation, repetition and mastery do not remove the risk, but they make it meaningful.

## Luck and meaning

The book's second line concerns luck. Taking a risk, on Pritchard's account, involves trusting to your luck; risk and luck are constitutive of an authentic and meaningful life. He also discusses what the framework implies for law, art and sport.

## A counter-reading

Any approach that separates risk from probability faces an obvious objection: in decision theory and in the practice of risk management, the calculus of probability is indispensable. Whether the fragility framework replaces those tools or answers a different question alongside them is one of the things the book will be argued over.`,
