    imageCredit: "Portrait of Blaise Pascal, Versailles · Wikimedia Commons",
    featured: false,
    seoTitle: "19 August: the death of Blaise Pascal",
    metaDescription: "Blaise Pascal died on 19 August 1662. The Pensées, the wager argument and 'the heart has its reasons'.",
    contentType: "HISTORY",
    sourceName: "List of philosophy anniversaries",
    sourceUrl: "https://en.everybodywiki.com/List_of_philosophy_anniversaries",
    publishedAt: "2026-08-19T05:00:00.000Z",
    authorSlug: "newsroom",
    categorySlug: "this-day-in-philosophy",
    tagSlugs: ["history", "philosophy-of-religion", "concepts"],
    philosopherSlugs: [],
    sources: [
      { title: "List of philosophy anniversaries", publisher: "Wikipedia / EverybodyWiki", url: "https://en.everybodywiki.com/List_of_philosophy_anniversaries" },
    ],
    content: `Blaise Pascal died in Paris on 19 August 1662, at the age of thirty-nine. Into that short life he fitted work in mathematics, physics and philosophy that looks, at first, unconnected.

## Mathematics and a machine

At sixteen Pascal wrote a treatise on conic sections. In his twenties he designed a mechanical calculator to ease the accounts of his father, a tax official. His experiments on the vacuum and air pressure unsettled the age's principle that nature abhors a vacuum.

His correspondence with Pierre de Fermat counts among the founding texts of probability theory. Arising from a question about gambling, that exchange made decision under uncertainty a subject for mathematics.

## The wager

Pascal's most argued-over philosophical argument grows out of exactly that thought about probability. The **wager** (*le pari*) does not try to prove the existence of God; it begins by accepting that proof is impossible.

The structure of the argument is a decision problem: if reason cannot settle the question, we still have to live, and in living we take a side. Pascal compares the possible gains and losses. Read today in the language of decision theory, the argument appears as an early instance of expected-utility reasoning — which is why it interests economists and decision theorists more than theologians.

A common misreading is that the wager says "you can choose to believe". Pascal knows belief cannot be produced by an act of will; his proposal is that a person put themselves into a way of living that leaves them open to belief.

## The reasons of the heart

The *Pensées* were compiled from the notes Pascal kept for an apologetic book he never finished. Fragmentary, sometimes a single sentence long, the text is counted among the summits of French prose despite its formal disorder.

Its best-known sentence is there: **"The heart has its reasons, which reason does not know."**

The sentence is often used as an anti-rationalist slogan; what Pascal says is subtler. First principles — space, time, number — are not known by proof but grasped directly. Reason builds its proofs on those direct apprehensions. The heart is not reason's enemy but its foundation.

## Between two infinities

Pascal's picture of the human follows from this: a being suspended between the infinitely large and the infinitely small, unable to grasp its own position. Yet within that weakness it carries a privilege — it is **"a thinking reed"**. The universe can crush it; but the universe does not know that it crushes, and the reed does.`,
