    content: `Writing about Hegel carries a standard occupational hazard: in the course of explaining Hegel, the explanation becomes as impenetrable as he is.

A few keywords are set out — thesis, antithesis, synthesis; Absolute Spirit; the dialectic; master and slave — and the file is closed.

In fact, one simple question is enough to get you into Hegel.

**How does a person become free?**

The whole system turns, in a manner of speaking, on that question. And the answer Hegel gives is the direct opposite of "leave me alone".

## The formula he never wrote

A correction first.

The formula "thesis-antithesis-synthesis" **does not appear** in Hegel's texts. The scheme is a schoolroom summary fastened onto him afterwards, and it distorts his thought quite as much as it simplifies it.

What Hegel does is to follow the tension inside a concept. Take a concept seriously and push it to the end, and it strikes its own limit and turns into something other than itself.

This is not a formula but a **method of reading**. And that is exactly why it cannot be reduced to a mechanical scheme.

## Recognition: the most modern concept

The liveliest vein in Hegel scholarship today is not the dialectic but **recognition** (*Anerkennung*).

The idea is this: a subject's consciousness of itself cannot be established single-handed.

Who I am, what I am capable of, what I am worth — I know these things only through **how others see me**. And in Hegel this is not an observation in psychology; it is a structural thesis.

The consequence is radical: **freedom is not independence from other people.**

Freedom is a person's capacity to realise his own will in the social world and, in the doing of it, to be **recognised** by others as a free subject.

The definition is a quiet criticism of the liberal conception of freedom. To be left alone is not to be free. In a world where nobody treats him as an interlocutor, a person may be unfree without meeting a single obstacle.

The [Axel Honneth feature](/news/axel-honneth-recognition-and-labour) we ran this week is the most systematic twenty-first-century continuation of the concept.

## Master and slave: why those pages still get read

These few pages of the *Phenomenology of Spirit* are among the most widely read in the history of philosophy.

The scene is this. Two self-consciousnesses meet. Each demands recognition from the other. A struggle to the death follows. One takes fright and submits: he becomes the slave. The other becomes the master.

The master appears to have won.

What Hegel shows is the reverse.

The master needs the slave's recognition — and the slave's recognition is **worthless**, because the slave has not been counted as a free subject. The master becomes dependent on the approval of someone to whom he ascribes no value.

The slave, for his part, **works**. He transforms nature. He sees his own labour in the thing he makes. And the moment he grasps that he can give the world a shape, his own consciousness is transformed.

This analysis is the direct ancestor of the theory of labour and alienation set out in the [Karl Marx feature](/news/marx-alienation-commodity-fetishism) we ran this week. Marx did not turn the passage on its head; he **materialised** it.

A warning: the section is not a historical narrative. Hegel is not describing an actual slave-holding society; he is analysing a logical moment of self-consciousness. To read the text as a sociological thesis is among the commonest mistakes.

## The charge of being the state's philosopher

For a long time Hegel was read in one way: the man who exalted the state, made history absolute and dissolved the individual into the whole — the intellectual forerunner of totalitarianism.

The reading had formidable defenders, and it was not entirely without ground: some formulations in the *Elements of the Philosophy of Right* are uncomfortable.

Post-war Hegel scholarship, though, changed the picture substantially.

The current Hegel entry in the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy stresses that the ethics and the political philosophy in particular can be read independently of the classical "metaphysical system" interpretation, and notes that more systematic and less metaphysical readings have gained ground in post-war work.

On this reading Hegel's concept of *Sittlichkeit* (ethical life) stands at the centre: the family, civil society and the state are not structures that crush the individual but institutional settings in which freedom can be **actualised**.

You are not obliged to agree. But it is worth knowing where the argument now stands.

## Where Hegel is in 2026

Work on Hegel today is no antiquarian pursuit.

From **16 to 18 October 2026** the **Hegel Society of America** holds its 28th biennial meeting at **Northwestern University** in Evanston, Illinois. The theme is ***Hegel and Time***.

The theme is no accident. Time is one of the hardest subjects in Hegel. Does history have a direction? What is the "now"? Is the self-realisation of Spirit a temporal process, or a logical structure?

Those questions intersect, unexpectedly, with the debates about grounding in analytic metaphysics that we followed this week in our [Sanders Prize in Metaphysics feature](/news/sanders-prize-metaphysics-2026-logical-atomism).

The same year has brought studies of Hegel's influence on the young Foucault — whose 1949 graduating thesis was on the *Phenomenology of Spirit*. That post-structuralist thought was built through a reckoning with Hegel is by now a sufficiently documented claim.

## Why read him?

Hegel is a hard writer, and there is nothing to be gained by softening the fact.

But there is a practical reason to read him. He neither reduces the institutions of the modern world — state, law, market, family — to individual preference, nor treats them as fixed natural structures.

He sees them as structures that are **historically constituted, carriers of contradiction and open to transformation**.

That view is a distant relative of the dual nature of law thesis described in the [Robert Alexy feature](/news/robert-alexy-dual-nature-of-law) we publish today: an institution is a fact, and at the same time something that must bear the burden of justifying itself.

Perhaps the strongest sentence Hegel leaves behind is this.

**Freedom is not the vanishing of contradictions; it is the capacity to realise oneself from within them.**

---

*Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel was born in Stuttgart on 27 August 1770 and died in Berlin on 14 November 1831. The cover image is Jakob Schlesinger's portrait of 1831; because Hegel lived before the invention of photography, a portrait from the period has been used.*`,
